    "referenceAnswerEn": "Criteria: under the Nine-Rank system family standing weighed heavily, and the rating officer’s judgement of pedigree commonly outweighed ability, producing a situation in which the high ranks held no men of humble origin and the low ranks no men of powerful families. The examination system selected on performance in written papers, and family background was not a formal criterion.\nControl: rating under the Nine-Rank system lay with local officers who were themselves usually drawn from local great families, so selection effectively rested with them. Under the examinations the court set the subjects, papers, marking and appointments centrally, leaving no local purchase.\nEffect on central authority: it cut the route by which great families monopolised office and weakened their political base; it tied an official’s entry and advancement to the court, shifting loyalty from clan to emperor and strengthening central control of the bureaucracy; and by opening office to men of humble origin it broadened the base of rule, drawing scholars to seek advancement through the system rather than through local power.",
    "contentEn": "State how the Sui-Tang civil examination system differed from the Nine-Rank system of the Wei-Jin period in its criteria for selection and in who controlled selection, and explain what the examination system did for central authority.",
    "explanationEn": "This is a comparison question, and marks depend on point-by-point correspondence — the two systems must be set against each other on the same dimension rather than described one after the other. The answer is divided into criteria and control because the question specifies those two dimensions; writing a paragraph on each system without matching them earns little even when the facts are sound.\nThe effect must be given as a mechanism, not a conclusion. \"It strengthened central authority\" is the conclusion; \"selection was taken into the court’s hands, advancement depended on the court, and loyalty therefore shifted from clan to emperor\" is the mechanism. Note that the examinations did not wholly displace hereditary privilege in the Tang, and the influence of the great families declined markedly only towards the end of the dynasty; writing that the examinations extinguished them at a stroke overstates the case."
